# Deny everything, then re-admit exactly what git tracks.
#
# Allowlist, not a denylist, and the reason is what could leak rather than
# image size. Docker reads none of .gitignore, .git/info/exclude or a global
# excludes file, so whatever keeps an untracked local file out of the repo
# does nothing to keep it out of the build context. A denylist would
# therefore protect exactly the files someone remembered to list on the day
# they wrote it, and every untracked file added afterwards would ship. The
# published image goes to two public registries, so a stray local file
# reaching it is not recoverable by deleting it afterwards.
#
# .env is here for the same structural reason and is the more immediate one:
# it holds real credentials, and `COPY . .` would bake it into a layer. The
# deny-all keeps the repo-root .env out, but it cannot reach a .env inside a
# directory admitted below, so .env and .env.* are also re-excluded by
# pattern at every depth at the bottom of this file. Only the repo-root
# .env.example is admitted back: a tracked template of defaults and
# placeholders, never real credentials. A nested .env.example stays
# excluded: none is tracked, nothing in the image reads one, and a file by
# that name outside git is as likely to hold real values as not.
#
# CI has never shipped any of this, because it builds from a git checkout
# where those files do not exist. That makes today's published image safe by
# accident. This file makes it safe by construction, and closes the gap for a
# local `docker build`, which would otherwise copy .env, .venv, .git and any
# untracked working directory in the checkout into the image.
#
# The admitted set below is `git ls-files` at the top level, minus tests/
# and the CI-only tooling files -- requirements-tooling.txt and its lock
# are installed by the workflow, never by the image.
*

!.env.example
!.github/
!.gitignore
!CHANGELOG.md
!CONTRIBUTING.md
!Dockerfile
!LICENSE
!PRIVACY.md
!README.md
!SECURITY.md
!alembic.ini
!app/
!constraints.txt
!docker-compose.backfill.yml
!docker-compose.backup.yml
!docker-compose.refresh.yml
!docker-compose.seeder.yml
!docker-compose.yml
!docker-entrypoint.sh
!libex_core/
!migrations/
!pytest.ini
!requirements-dev.lock
!requirements-dev.txt
!requirements.lock
!requirements.txt
!scripts/

# tests/ is deliberately NOT admitted. Nothing ever ran it from inside the
# image: tests.yml runs pytest on the runner against the checkout, and
# publish.yml only builds and pushes, gated on tests.yml having already
# passed. The test runner itself was removed from the image when this line
# was added, so shipping the tests without pytest would have left
# 952K of code the image cannot execute.

# Re-excluded after the admissions above, since these live inside admitted
# directories and are untracked build or tooling residue. app/static/docs is
# fetched during the build by fetch_docs_assets.sh and checksum-verified
# there, so a copy of it from the build host would be overwritten anyway --
# and must not be trusted in the meantime.
**/__pycache__/
**/*.py[cod]
app/static/docs/

# Credential files, excluded wherever they sit. **/ matches the top level as
# well, which is why the repo-root .env.example has to be admitted again on
# the last line: a later match wins, so moving it above these would exclude it.
**/.env
**/.env.*
!.env.example
